from ./LanguageData import LanguageData
from ./Configuration import Configuration

export anchor Determinism:
    description:
        Analysis is deterministic. Given the same inputs, it produces the same
        semantic model, the same measures and the same instructions, byte for
        byte, on any machine, every time. Without this, a requirement could
        change between two runs with nobody having changed the
        specification, and nothing analysis says could be trusted.

    inputs:
        These are all of the inputs. Nothing else can change the result.

        ```text
        the analyzed files, and every file they import
        the version of the Piton compiler, which fixes the language rules
        the version of the analyzer, which fixes the rules in this specification
        the project's analysis configuration
        the language data: the lists in this specification, any project
          additions, and the dictionary's name and version
        the arguments the command was run with
        ```

        Every report records each of them, and a fingerprint computed from
        all of them together, so two reports can be compared only when their
        fingerprints say they were produced the same way. The language data is
        described in @{LanguageData}, the configuration in @{Configuration}.

    whatItRulesOut:
        ```text
        generative models         no language model is asked anything
        statistical choices       no result is chosen because it is likely
        the network               nothing is fetched while analysis runs
        the clock                 no result depends on the time, and reports
                                  carry no timestamps
        randomness                nothing is sampled or shuffled
        the file system's order   files are always processed in path order
        parallel timing           work may run in parallel, but results are
                                  always put in the same order
        ```

    explainable:
        Being repeatable is not enough. A statistical word tagger with fixed
        weights gives the same answer every time, but it cannot say why, and
        analysis must be able to say why. So every result in the model names
        what produced it: a list entry, a dictionary entry, a pattern, or a
        rule in this specification. Anything that cannot do that is outside
        analysis, however repeatable it is.

    theBoundary:
        Some useful tools are probabilistic by nature, such as a model that
        suggests what an unrecognized statement might mean. They are not part
        of analysis, and nothing in this specification uses one.

        If one is ever added beside analysis, it stays on the other side of a
        fixed boundary. It may read the semantic model. Its output is always
        labeled as a suggestion and shown separately. It never becomes a fact,
        an open item, a measure, or an instruction, and it never changes the
        command's exit status. A suggestion becomes part of the specification
        only when a person writes it into the specification.

    whyConservative:
        Wrong certainty is worse than an honest unknown. An unknown is
        reported, and someone decides it. A wrong fact is built, tested
        against, and shipped, and nothing points back to where it went wrong.
        So whenever a rule would have to assume something to produce a
        result, analysis produces an open item instead.

    numbers:
        Measures are ratios of whole-number counts. They are computed exactly
        and rounded half to even, to two decimal places, only when shown.
